  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "The specimen is strong on byte-level envelope discipline: deterministic CBOR, opaque payload bytes, and an explicit unknown-pCID reject rule. That helps avoid unsafe parsing and makes failure visible, but it does not directly answer the scenario's unknown-CAS-type requirement. Unknown CID-codec behavior is delegated to the payload protocol, and unknown envelope handlers are hard-rejected rather than safely stored, advertised, and forwarded across mixed-version networks.",
    "strengths": [
      "Keeps payload opaque until the pCID-selected handler runs, reducing unsafe envelope-layer parsing.",
      "Makes unknown pCID failure explicit and locally observable instead of silently guessing.",
      "Deterministic CBOR and a fixed signed prefix support reproducible audit of transmitted bytes."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The scenario asks about unknown CID codecs, but this spec only defines unknown pCID behavior at the envelope layer.",
      "Hard reject blocks valid-message storage, advertisement, and forwarding when the envelope type is unknown.",
      "No standard quarantine or recovery path is defined for later interpretation of rejected data."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Mixed-version networks can strand newer object or protocol types instead of preserving them opaquely for later upgrade.",
      "Different payload protocols may invent inconsistent unknown-CID behavior because the envelope leaves that rule unspecified."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "If Dave knows the envelope pCID but not an inner CID codec, what exact local actions are allowed?",
      "Should unknown-pCID envelopes have a quarantine mode for storage, advertisement, or forwarding as opaque bytes?",
      "How would later upgraded peers recover and re-verify previously rejected data?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
